  "text": ".getFont[]\nLet's analyze the conditions given for the polynomial \\( P(x) \\).\n\n1. \\( P(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 3.\n2. The roots of \\( P(x) \\) are in arithmetic progression.\n3. The roots are real and positive.\n4. The coefficients of \\( P(x) \\) are integers.\n\nSince the roots are in arithmetic progression, let's denote the roots as \\( a-d, a, a+d \\).\nGiven that one of the roots is 3, there are three possible cases:\n\n#### Case 1: The root is \\( a-d \\).\n\\[ a-d = 3 \\]\nSince the roots are in arithmetic progression, \\( a = 0 \\) and \\( a+d = 3 \\):\n\\[ 0 + d = 3 \\Rightarrow d = 3 \\]\nThus, the roots in this case are \\( 3, 0, 3 \\).\n\n#### Case 2: The root is \\( a \\).\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\nSince the roots are in arithmetic progression, \\( a-d = 0 \\) and \\( a+d = 6 \\):\n\\[ 3 - d = 0 \\Rightarrow d = 3 \\]\nThus, the roots in this case are \\( 0, 3, 6 \\).\n\n#### Case 3: The root is \\( a+d \\).\n\\[ a+d = 3 \\]\nSince the roots are in arithmetic progression, \\( a = 3 \\) and \\( a-d = 0 \\):\n\\[ 3 - d = 0 \\Rightarrow d = 3 \\]\nThus, the roots in this case are \\( -3, 3, 9 \\).\n\nNext, we determine the coefficient of \\( P(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\). Since \\( P(x) \\) has real coefficients:\n- For \\( 3, 0, 3 \\):\n\\[ P(x) = (x-3)x(x-3) = (x-3)^2 x \\]\nHere, \\( \\text{coeff}(x) = 0 \\).\n\n- For \\( 0, 3, 6 \\):\n\\[ P(x) = x(x^2 - 9) = x(x^2 - 9) \\]\nHere, \\( \\text{coeff}(x) = 1 \\).\n\n- For \\( -3, 3, 9 \\):\n\\[ P(x) = (x+3)(x-3)(x-9) = (x^2 - 9)(x-9) \\]\nHere, \\( \\text{coeff}(x) = -9 \\).\n\nThe coefficients for the roots in arithmetic progression are not integers. Therefore, there are no such polynomials \\( P(x) \\) that satisfy all the given conditions.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{0}\\)",
